<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Book of the Later Han</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">5th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Hou Hanshu, History of the Later Han, Hou Han shu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Book of the Later Han (Hou Hanshu) is the official history of the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> (25–220 CE), compiled by the historian Fan Ye under the Liu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>. Fan Ye wrote its annals and biographies before his execution in 445, and its treatises were later taken from Sima Biao's earlier history. It is one of the Twenty-Four Histories and a key source on Han relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/book-of-the-later-han.jpg" alt="Book of the Later Han" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 范曄 (Fan Ye, 398–445) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%BE%8C%E6%BC%A2%E6%9B%B8%EF%BC%88%E5%AE%8B%E7%B4%B9%E8%88%88%EF%BC%89.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
